"Decoupling Safety into Orthogonal Subspace: Cost-Efficient and Performance-Preserving Alignment for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09004v1)\nciteturn2858view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09004v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 526\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 The Dilemma of Safety Alignment: Data Composition for Balancing Harmlessness and Helpfulness L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Impact of General-Purpose Data Selection L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Impact of General-Purpose Data Proportion L20:   4. cite10†3 LoRA for Cost-Efficient, Performance-Preserving, and Plug-and-Play Safety Alignment L21:     1. cite11†3.1 Advantages of LoRA-Based over Full-Parameter Safety Alignment L22:     2. cite12†3.2 Plug-and-Play Safety Patching with LoRA in Lifelong Learning L23:   5. cite13†4 Theoretical Explanation L24:   6. cite14†5 Experimental Analyses L25:     1. cite15†5.1 The magnitude of parameter updates ($|\\Delta W|$) L26:     2. cite16†5.2 The layer-wise changes in hidden states ($\\Delta h^{(l)}$) L27:     3. cite17†5.3 Orthogonality of Safety Updates L28:     4. cite18†5.4 Cross-Domain Analysis of LoRA: Safety Subspace Distinctiveness L29:     5. cite19†5.5 The effect of LoRA rank on safety alignment L30:     6. cite20†5.6 On the Relationship Between LoRA Rank and SVD Decomposition L31:   7. cite21†6 Discussion L32:   8. cite22†7 Related Work L33:   9. cite23†8 Conclusion L34:   10. cite24†References L35:   11. cite25†A Training Datasets L36:     1. cite26†A.1 Safety-critical Data L37:     2. cite27†A.2 General-purpose Data L38:   12. cite28†B Details for Evaluation L39:     1. cite29†B.1 Statistics of Evaluation Sets L40:     2. cite30†B.2 Safety Evaluation L41:     3. cite31†B.3 General Evaluation L42:   13. cite32†C Implementation Details L43:   14. cite33†D Scaling LoRA Safety Patches to Larger LLMs L44:   15. cite34†E Transformations in Orthogonal Subspaces: A Derivation L45:     1. cite35†E.1 Orthogonal case L46:     2. cite36†E.2 Non-orthogonal case L47:   16. cite37†F Case Study L48: cite38†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L49: \nL50: arXiv:2510.09004v1 [cs.CL] 10 Oct 2025\nL51: # Decoupling Safety into Orthogonal Subspace: Cost-Efficient and Performance-Preserving Alignment for Large Language Models\nL52: Yutao Mou Affiliation: National Engineering Research Center for Software Engineering, Peking University, China{yutao.mou, xiaolingzhou}@stu.pku.edu.cn, {zhangsk,wye}@pku.edu.cn    Xiaoling Zhou Affiliation: National Engineering Research Center for Software Engineering, Peking University, China{yutao.mou, xiaolingzhou}@stu.pku.edu.cn, {zhangsk,wye}@pku.edu.cn    Yuxiao Luo Affiliation: National Engineering Research Center for Software Engineering, Peking University, China{yutao.mou, xiaolingzhou}@stu.pku.edu.cn, {zhangsk,wye}@pku.edu.cn    Shikun Zhang Affiliation: National Engineering Research Center for Software Engineering, Peking University, China{yutao.mou, xiaolingzhou}@stu.pku.edu.cn, {zhangsk,wye}@pku.edu.cn    Wei Ye ^{†}^{†}thanks: corresponding author.\nL53: Affiliation: National Engineering Research Center for Software Engineering, Peking University, China{yutao.mou, xiaolingzhou}@stu.pku.edu.cn, {zhangsk,wye}@pku.edu.cn\nL54: ###### Abstract\nL55: Safety alignment is essential for building trustworthy artificial intelligence, yet it remains challenging to enhance model safety without degrading general performance. Current approaches require computationally expensive searches for the optimal proportion of safety-critical and general-purpose data to balance safety and general performance, incurring high costs with limited gains.\nL56: In this work, we show that LoRA-based Refusal-training enables performance-preserving safety alignment even when trained solely on safety data, demonstrating that LoRA serves as cost-efficient, performance-preserving, and plug-and-play safety patches.\nL57: Beyond empirical findings, we provide both theoretical and experimental evidence that LoRA effectively decouples safety into a low-rank subspace largely orthogonal to the model’s intrinsic transformation space, ensuring that safety enhancements do not interfere with inherent capabilities.^{1}^{1} 1 We release our code at cite39†https://github.com/MurrayTom/LoRA4Safe†github.com . Warning: this paper includes examples that may be offensive or harmful.\nL58: ## 1 Introduction\nL59: With the rapid progress of large language models (LLMs) (cite40†Hurst et al., 2024 ; cite41†Dubey et al., 2024 ; cite42†Yang et al., 2024a ), ensuring that they do not generate harmful content (cite43†Mo et al., 2023 ; cite44†Ji et al., 2024b ) or execute malicious operations (cite45†Bhatt et al., 2023 ; cite46†Yuan et al., 2024 ; cite47†Mou et al., 2025a ) has become a critical challenge.\nL60: To address this, safety alignment techniques, such as supervised fine-tuning (SFT) (cite48†Bianchi et al., 2023 ; cite49†Choi et al., 2024 ), reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) (cite50†Dai et al., 2023 ; cite51†Ouyang et al., 2022 ), and direct preference optimization (DPO) (cite52†Rafailov et al., 2024 ; cite53†Mou et al., 2025b ), have been widely adopted to align model behavior with human values, forming the foundation for safe and trustworthy AI deployment.\nL61: However, existing approaches often fall short in generalizing to “unseen” jailbreak attacks encountered in real-world settings (cite54†Wei et al., 2023 ), while simultaneously degrading the general capabilities of LLMs (e.g., knowledge QA, mathematical reasoning, and code generation) (cite55†Wei et al., 2024a ; cite56†Huang et al., 2025 ).\nL62: Recent efforts have increasingly centered on post-training approaches for aligning LLMs with safety objectives, including refusal-oriented supervised fine-tuning (Refusal-SFT) (cite57†Ge et al., 2024 ), preference-based optimization (cite58†Ji et al., 2024a ; cite59†Diao et al., 2024 ), representation-level interventions (cite60†Zou et al., 2024 ; cite61†Lu et al., 2025 ), and reasoning-aware alignment techniques (cite53†Mou et al., 2025b ; cite62†Zhang et al., 2025 ).\nL63: Our preliminary experiments show that post-training alignment methods are highly sensitive to the composition of training data, requiring a delicate balance between safety-critical and general-purpose examples (Section cite7†2 ). Identifying the optimal data composition is costly, as both the proportion and distribution of data strongly affect the trade-off between safety and general performance.\nL64: Moreover, in practical deployment, red-teaming and safety alignment are typically conducted in alternating iterative cycles (cite57†Ge et al., 2024 ; cite59†Diao et al., 2024 ), where the challenges of data composition become even more pronounced in continuous learning settings.\nL65: cite63†Image: Refer to caption L66: \nL67: Figure 1: (a) LoRA-based SFT achieves better safety–utility trade-off than full-parameter training. (b) Schematic illustration of transformation spaces induced by LoRA (right) and full-parameter (left) training. The $\\Delta W$ subspace from LoRA training is orthogonal to the model’s $W_{0}$ subspace, avoiding interference, while full-parameter training produces non-orthogonal and interfering subspaces.\nL68: Safety alignment often comes at the cost of degraded general capabilities, essentially manifesting as a form of “catastrophic forgetting” (cite64†Parisi et al., 2018 ; cite65†Zhong et al., 2023 ; cite66†Wang et al., 2024b ). Recent work has introduced a variety of LoRA-based methods, such as O-LoRA (cite67†Wang et al., 2023 ), SD-LoRA (cite68†Wu et al., 2025b ), and I-LoRA (cite69†Ren et al., 2024 ) to alleviate knowledge forgetting in multi-task lifelong learning.\nL69: In this work, we show that LoRA-based refusal training markedly enhances safety while preserving performance, without relying on general-purpose data. This highlights LoRA’s strong potential for safety alignment.”\nL70: In the context of safety alignment, LoRA has three key advantages: (1) Performance-preserving: LoRA-based Refusal-SFT substantially reduces jailbreak attack success rates while maintaining general capabilities (Figure cite70†1 (a)); (2) Cost-efficient: training LoRA weights solely on safety data achieves a better safety–utility trade-off than full-parameter fine-tuning with carefully balanced data composition, significantly lowering alignment cost; (3) Plug-and-play (Section cite12†3.2 ): as lightweight and modular components, LoRA safety patches enable efficient and continual mitigation of safety vulnerabilities in lifelong alignment (cite71†Wang et al., 2025 ).\nL71: These properties position LoRA as an effective safety patch for LLMs (Section cite10†3 ).\nL72: To understand why LoRA can serve as an effective safety patch for LLMs, we combine theoretical insights with empirical evidence. From the theoretical perspective (Section cite13†4 ), we consider a weight matrix $W_{0}$ of the original model and a low-rank update $\\Delta W$ introduced by LoRA.\nL73: Applying singular value decomposition (SVD) (cite72†Klema & Laub, 1980 ; cite73†Wall et al., 2003 ) to a weight matrix decomposes its transformations into orthogonal basis vectors, which represent the principal directions of the input space shaped by the model. The more orthogonal the subspace spanned by $\\Delta W$ is to that of $W_{0}$, the more independently their induced transformations can operate, with reduced interference (Figure cite70†1 (b)).\nL74: This theoretical view motivates our empirical analyses (Section cite14†5 ), which quantitatively examine three aspects: (i) parameter update magnitude, (ii) layer-wise hidden state shifts, and (iii) the orthogonality between LoRA-induced safety subspace and the model’s inherent transformation subspace.\nL75: Our results show that LoRA-based refusal-training decouples safety into a low-rank subspace that is largely orthogonal to the original model’s transformations, thereby enhancing safety without compromising general capabilities. Furthermore, through a comparative analysis of fine-tuning on data of code and finance domain, we observe that LoRA-based safety fine-tuning exerts the least adverse impact on general performance.\nL76: Notably, the orthogonality between the subspace of safety-related parameter shifts and the model’s inherent transformations is more pronounced, which further substantiates the distinctive benefits of LoRA-based refusal training.\nL77: We summarize the main contributions of this study as follows:\nL78: \nL79:   * •\nL80: \nL81: LoRA as Safety Patches. We demonstrate that LoRA can serve as cost-efficient, performance-preserving, and plug-and-play safety patches for LLMs, substantially reducing jailbreak success rates while largely maintaining general capabilities.\nL82: \nL83:   * •\nL84: Theoretical Insight. We introduce transformation subspace orthogonality as the theoretical explanation, which helps to interpret why LoRA-based safety alignment can achieve improved safety without interfering with core abilities.\nL85: \nL86:   * •\nL87: Empirical Evidence. Through quantitative analyses, we show that LoRA-based Refusal-training constructs a safety subspace orthogonal to the model’s intrinsic transformations. Cross-domain comparisons indicate that the safety subspace is more orthogonal and minimally impacts general performance compared to other domains, which sheds light on promising research directions for safety alignment.\nL88: ## 2 The Dilemma of Safety Alignment: Data Composition for Balancing Harmlessness and Helpfulness\nL89: Current mainstream post-training safety alignment methods typically require the mixture of safety-critical and general-purpose data (cite59†Diao et al., 2024 ; cite60†Zou et al., 2024 ; cite74†Qi et al., 2024 ). The preservation of general capabilities is highly sensitive to both the selection (quality and distribution) and the proportion (quantity and ratio) of general-purpose data (cite75†Dong et al., 2023 ; cite76†Zhou et al., 2023 ).\nL90: In this section, we present empirical studies illustrating how general-purpose data selection and proportion affect the safety–utility trade-off in LLM alignment.\nL91: ### 2.1 Impact of General-Purpose Data Selection\nL92: \nL93: (a) Qwen2.5-7B-IT\nL94: \nL95: (b) LLAMA3.1-8B-IT\nL96: \nL97: Figure 2: Impact of different choices of general-purpose data in Refusal-SFT on LLM safety and general capabilities. Higher scores indicate better performance, yet achieving an optimal balance between safety and general performance remains challenging.\nL98: Firstly, we examine how the selection of general-purpose data mixed with safety-critical data affects the safety–utility trade-off in alignment. Specifically, we use SafeEdit-Train (cite66†Wang et al., 2024b ) as safety-critical data and combine it with general-purpose samples from OpenOrca (cite77†Mukherjee et al., 2023 ), LIMA (cite76†Zhou et al., 2023 ), and Ultra-Chat (cite78†Ding et al., 2023 ) to construct three training sets (details in Appendix cite25†A ).\nL99: We then perform full-parameter SFT on Qwen2.5-7B-IT (cite79†Yang et al., 2024b ) and LLAMA3.1-8B-IT (cite41†Dubey et al., 2024 ). Results are shown in Figure cite80†2 .\nL100: In full-parameter fine-tuning, simultaneously ensuring both safety and usefulness through data composition is highly challenging. We observe that, regardless of which general-purpose dataset is combined with safety data, larger safety improvements from Refusal-SFT come at the cost of greater degradation in general capabilities. Specifically, mixing LIMA with SafeEdit-Train results in the largest loss in general performance but achieves the highest safety gains.\nL101: In contrast, using OpenOrca as the general-purpose dataset minimizes the drop in general capabilities, while the corresponding improvement in safety is relatively smaller.\nL102: ### 2.2 Impact of General-Purpose Data Proportion\nL103: \nL104: (a) Safety (Lower ASR, Better models)\nL105: \nL106: (b) General Performance\nL107: \nL108: Figure 3: Effect of different general-purpose data ratios during Refusal-SFT training on safety and general capabilities of LLMs. Varying the proportion of general-purpose data reveals an inherent trade-off between safety and general performance.\nL109: Next, we analyze how the ratio between general-purpose and safety data in the training set influences the safety–utility trade-off of aligned models. We use 4K $\\langle\\text{harmful query},\\text{safe response}\\rangle$ pairs from SafeEdit-Train as safety data, and sample 4K, 8K, 12K, 16K, and 20K instances from OpenOrca as general data. We fine-tuned LLMs with full-parameter SFT, and results are shown in Figure cite81†3 .\nL110: Overall, adjusting the proportion of general-purpose data presents a trade-off between safety and general performance. Increasing the share of general-purpose data can alleviate the loss in general capabilities but reduces the safety gains, whereas decreasing it further enhances safety at the expense of greater general performance degradation. These results highlight the inherent challenge of balancing harmlessness and helpfulness in full-parameter safety fine-tuning.\nL111: ## 3 LoRA for Cost-Efficient, Performance-Preserving, and Plug-and-Play Safety Alignment\nL112: \nL113: cite82†Image: Refer to caption L114: \nL115: Figure 4: LoRA for safety alignment: (a) cost-efficient and performance-preserving alternative to full-parameter fine-tuning; (b) plug-and-play safety patching in multi-round red-teaming and continuous learning.\nL116: ### 3.1 Advantages of LoRA-Based over Full-Parameter Safety Alignment\nL117: There are three common paradigms for LLM safety alignment: (1) supervised fine-tuning on malicious instructions with safe responses (Refusal-SFT) (cite57†Ge et al., 2024 ); (2) direct preference optimization (DPO) with harmful/harmless response pairs (cite59†Diao et al., 2024 ; cite42†Yang et al., 2024a ); and (3) reinforcement learning (RL) with safety rewards (cite58†Ji et al., 2024a ). To balance safety and utility, these methods usually mix safety-critical and general-purpose instructions.\nL118: However, as we discuss in the last section, achieving safety–utility trade-off through data composition remains difficult.\nL119: We consider the loss of general capabilities in safety alignment as a form of \"catastrophic forgetting\" (cite64†Parisi et al., 2018 ; cite83†Song et al., 2023 ; cite84†Zheng et al., 2024a ; cite85†Song et al., 2025 ). Recent LoRA variants (e.g., O-LoRA (cite67†Wang et al., 2023 ), SD-LoRA (cite68†Wu et al., 2025b ), I-LoRA (cite69†Ren et al., 2024 )) mitigate knowledge forgetting by isolating domain knowledge (medical, legal, financial) into separate adapters.\nL120: By analogy, safety can be treated as another domain, motivating the question: Does LoRA-based safety alignment better balance safety and general performance than full fine-tuning?\nL121: To investigate this, we compare different alignment methods (Refusal-SFT and DPO) under both full-parameter and LoRA fine-tuning. The former considers both \"Safety Only\" and \"Safety–General Mixture\" data configuration, while LoRA adopts \"Safety Only\" data (Figure cite86†4 (a)). For malicious data, we build instruction-tuning and preference sets from 4K jailbreak samples in SafeEdit-Train (cite66†Wang et al., 2024b ).\nL122: For general-purpose data, we use 12K OpenOrca samples (cite77†Mukherjee et al., 2023 ) for SFT and 12K helpful-base preference pairs from HH-RLHF (cite87†Bai et al., 2022 ) for DPO. Further dataset and evaluation details are in Appendix cite25†A and cite28†B .\nL123: Surprisingly, we find that LoRA-based Refusal-SFT trained solely on <malicious instruction, safe response> pairs achieves optimal trade-off between safety and general performance, without requiring any general-purpose data for training. As shown in Table cite88†1 , we can draw three key findings:\nL124: \nL125:   * •\nL126: SFT yields stronger safety gains than DPO but incurs higher general performance loss. For instance, with full-parameter fine-tuning of Qwen2.5-7B-IT on the safety–general mixture dataset, Refusal-SFT reduces jailbreak success rates to near zero on jailbreak tests of SG-Bench and SaladBench but substantially degrades open-ended generation and reasoning. In contrast, DPO largely preserves general capabilities but leaves the model more vulnerable to jailbreaks. LoRA fine-tuning yields similar conclusions.\nL127: We further analyze the underlying causes in Section cite15†5.1 .\nL128:   * •\nL129: \nL130: Full-parameter fine-tuning on safety data may severely degrade performance, while mixing general data compromises safety. Under the \"Safety only“ data configuration, both Refusal-SFT and DPO markedly improve jailbreak defense but cause severe performance degradation. For instance, LLAMA3.1-8B-IT and Mistral-7B-IT see MMLU scores drop to nearly zero. Manual inspection further reveals that these aligned models frequently over-refuse benign instructions.\nL131: \nL132:   * •\nL133: LoRA-based Refusal-SFT substantially improves safety with minimal impact on general performance even using only safety data. Compared with full-parameter fine-tuning, LoRA offers three key advantages: (1) strong defense against unseen jailbreaks (ASR near zero); (2) minimal effect on general capabilities; and (3) training requires only red-teaming data and tuning a few external parameters, making it simple and efficient.\nL134: In summary, we believe that LoRA can be used as a cost-efficient and performance-preserving safety patch for LLMs.\nL135: \nL136: Table 1: Comparison of LoRA-based and full-parameter safety alignment across different models. We focus on two common safety alignment methods: Refusal-SFT and DPO. marks the best trade-off between safety and performance (maximized safety under tolerable performance loss). Percentages indicate changes relative to the instruct model ($|\\Delta|<2\\%$ are labeled as “$\\approx$No Loss.”)\nL137: Model  | Safety (↓)  | Open-end Gen. (↑)  | Knowledge (↑)  | MATH (↑)  | Code (↑)\nL138: WildJailbreak  | SG-Bench  | SaladBench  | MT-Bench  | MMLU  | MATH-500  | HumanEval\nL139: Qwen2.5-7B-IT Models\nL140: Instruct Model (baseline)  | 40.05  | 36.63  | 34.46  | 7.65  | 70.43  | 71.2  | 80.94\nL141: Refusal-SFT\nL142: Full-parameter (safety only)  | 0.05 (↓99.88%)  | 0.08 (↓99.78%)  | 0.08 (↓99.77%)  | 6.64 (-13.20%)  | 67.22 (-4.56%)  | 60.40 (-15.17%)  | 79.69 (-1.54%)\nL143: Full-parameter (safety-general)  | 16.25 (↓59.43%)  | 2.26 (↓93.83%)  | 2.06 (↓94.02%)  | 6.80 (-11.11%)  | 68.84 (-2.26%)  | 61.40 (-13.76%)  | 79.94 (-1.24%)\nL144: LoRA-based (safety only)  | 7.80 (↓80.52%)  | 0.85 (↓97.68%)  | 1.24 (↓96.40%)  | 7.64 (-0.13%)  | 69.47 (-1.36%)  | 70.20 (-1.40%)  | 81.16 (+0.27%)\nL145: DPO\nL146: Full-parameter (safety only)  | 15.95 (↓60.16%)  | 1.40 (↓96.18%)  | 2.74 (↓92.05%)  | 7.64 (-0.13%)  | 69.98 (-0.64%)  | 66.40 (-6.74%)  | 80.55 (-0.48%)\nL147: Full-parameter (safety-general)  | 29.95 (↓25.19%)  | 10.46 (↓71.44%)  | 11.74 (↓65.91%)  | 7.66 (+0.13%)  | 70.50 (+0.10%)  | 69.20 (-2.81%